# Documentation style

Every document describes the system **as it is now**. A reader arriving today
must not have to reconstruct the present from a history of changes.

## Write the current state

- Present tense, active voice, stated as fact: "Renewing inserts a new policy
  year", not "we changed renewal so that it now inserts".
- No changelogs, no version history, no "recently", "now", "previously", "as of
  v0.2", "the old approach", "we used to".
- Edit in place. When behaviour changes, replace the paragraph that described the
  old behaviour rather than appending a correction next to it.
- Do not narrate the work: no "added", "updated", "improved", "refactored". That
  belongs in the commit message.

## Be assertive and concrete

- Commit to a statement. Prefer "Backups are encrypted with the same key" over
  "backups should generally be encrypted".
- Quote real values — 30 days, 300 issues, 64 MiB, `CL-00001` — not "a
  configurable threshold". Take them from the code.
- Name the file or symbol a reader would open next.
- Explain the reasoning behind a design decision, not just the mechanics. Why a
  policy year is never mutated matters more than which columns it has.

## Be honest about what is not built

Unbuilt features are stated plainly in the present tense: "Reminder sending is
not built; the settings and templates are seeded and editable." Never imply that
scaffolding works, and never hedge it into vagueness either.

## Structure

- Lead with the answer, then the detail.
- Tables for enumerable facts; prose for reasoning.
- Reference code with backticked paths (`src-tauri/src/repo/policies.rs`).
- Mermaid for diagrams, so they render on GitHub and stay editable.
